"posterior": 0.143, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 1}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2.1.2", "B2.2.1", "B2.2.2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=57%, source=both]\n\nB-Cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-ALL) vs T-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma with Mediastinal Mass:\n  Favours first:  balance problems, bleeding from the gums, blurred vision, bone pain, enlarged liver\n\nB-Cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-ALL) vs Lymphoblastic Lymphoma with Philadelphia Chromosome (Ph+ Variant):\n  Favours first:  balance problems, bleeding from the gums, blurred vision, bone pain, enlarged liver\n\nT-Cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL) vs T-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma with Mediastinal Mass:\n  Favours first:  balance problems, bleeding from the gums, blurred vision, bone pain, enlarged liver\n\nT-Cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL) vs Lymphoblastic Lymphoma with Philadelphia Chromosome (Ph+ Variant):\n  Favours first:  balance problems, bleeding from the gums, blurred vision, bone pain, enlarged liver\n\nRelated to B-Cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-ALL): acute lymphoblastic leukemia (disease), acute lymphoblastic/lymphocytic leukemia, childhood leukemia\n\nRelated to T-Cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL): acute lymphoblastic leukemia (disease), acute lymphoblastic/lymphocytic leukemia, childhood leukemia\n\nRelated to T-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma with Mediastinal Mass: childhood precursor t-lymphoblastic lymphoma/leukemia, lymphoblastic lymphoma, pediatric lymphoma\n\nRelated to Lymphoblastic Lymphoma with Philadelphia Chromosome (Ph+ Variant): non-hodgkin lymphoma, precursor lymphoblastic lymphoma/leukemia, t-cell childhood lymphoblastic lymphoma\n\n[RAG context for 'A 57-year-old man presents to the emergency department with several days of malaise' (score=0.55):]\n  Source: Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia in Children. > Prognosis\n  T-ALL shows an unfavorable prognosis compared to B-ALL. Early T-cell precursor ALL, a rare subtype of T-ALL/LBL, is particularly associated with an unfavorable outcome. Overall, T-LBL prognosis depend\n[RAG context for 'A 57-year-old man presents to the emergency department with several days of malaise' (score=0.59):]\n  Source: Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia in Children. > Epidemiology\n  The estimated number of ALL new cases in the United States is approximately 6000 per year. ALL is primarily a disease of children younger than 6 years with a slight male predominance. About 85% of ALL\n[RAG context for 'weakness' (score=0.54):]\n  Source: Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia in Children. > Prognosis\n  T-ALL shows an unfavorable prognosis compared to B-ALL. Early T-cell precursor ALL, a rare subtype of T-ALL/LBL, is particularly associated with an unfavorable outcome. Overall, T-LBL prognosis depend\n[RAG context for 'weakness' (score=0.58):]\n  Source: Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia in Children. > Pathophysiology\n  B-ALL is classified into 2 distinct entities:\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  headache with blurry vision → central nervous system (CNS) involvement in ALL → B-Cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-ALL)\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for CNS infiltration (e.g., fundoscopic exam, MRI brain, CSF analysis) to confirm ALL-related complications\n  weight loss over the past month → B symptoms (systemic symptoms of weight loss, night sweats, fever) → Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Assess for additional B symptoms and consider lymphoma/plasma cell disorder in the differential\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
